The program pulls every `name <- value` assignment out of standard input with a regular expression and runs them in order. It then prints the final values of `a`, `b` and `TMP` in the required format. It skips any surrounding text that is not an assignment, so the whole message can be piped in safely.

```python
import re
import sys

def main():
    text = sys.stdin.read()
    env = {}
    pattern = re.compile(r'([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*<-\s*(-?\d+|[A-Za-z_]\w*)')
    for target, source in pattern.findall(text):
        if re.fullmatch(r'-?\d+', source):
            env[target] = int(source)
        else:
            env[target] = env.get(source)
    names = ['a', 'b', 'TMP']
    print(' '.join(f"{n} ?= {env.get(n)};" for n in names))

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```
